  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I didn't open the dashboard or check that the rule runs.\n\n**Data:** The Kibana Sample Data Logs view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`) has `timestamp` as its time field. Its useful fields are `response` (a string, with a `.keyword` version), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `url` and `request`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-56041979 Web logs overview\" has four panels. Its URL is http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-56041979-web-logs-overview-dabfeca6d020.\n- **Total requests:** a single-number count of all requests.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart of request count by `timestamp`.\n- **Responses by status code:** a pie chart split by `response.keyword`.\n- **Total bytes by file extension:** a bar chart of summed `bytes` by `extension.keyword`.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-56041979 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It looks back over 5 minutes.\n- It counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*`.\n- It fires when that count is above 10.\n\nRules are created disabled by default, so I enabled this one to let it actually fire. It has no actions attached, so it will only record alerts and won't send notifications.",
